Analyze the following GitHub issue and classify it into one of these categories:

PROJECT GOAL: We aim to verify if the user needs to set up a build environment to resolve the issue:

1. Does not need build environment
   (The solutions/answers can be validated without build environment, e.g., it's a conceptual issue, documentation issue, or can be verified with simple code analysis)

2. Can be dockerized without any issue
   (The solutions must require a build environment to be verified)

3. Requires build environment but hard to be dockerized or cannot be reproduced now
   (The solution must require a build environment, but it is difficult to containerize for reasons such as: hardware dependencies, network dependencies, external API access with authentication, timing-dependent behaviors, requiring MacOS, multiple users interacting simultaneously, licensed software, etc.)

IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
- Choose category 1 whenever possible. If the LLM can analyze the issue and verify the solution without needing to build or run the code, always select category 1.
- Only classify an issue as requiring a build environment (categories 2 or 3) when it's ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to actually build and run the code to verify if the solution works.

GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2016-05-12T10:28:40Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: BackPressure and nested flatMap
Description: Hi, can someone explain why the following code (without nested flatmap)

<div class="highlight highlight-source-java"><pre>
Observable.from(new IterateOverFloat(1))) //
                .flatMap(__ -> Observable.create(new OnSubscribeFromIterable<>(new IterateOverFloat(10_000_000)))) //
                .flatMap(Observable::just) //
                .observeOn(Schedulers.computation()) //
                .subscribe();
</pre></div>

goes through method `slowPath(long)` of `OnSubscribeFromIterable.IterableProducer`,

while the following code (with nested flat map)

<div class="highlight highlight-source-java"><pre>
Observable.from(new IterateOverFloat(1)) //
                .flatMap(__ -> Observable.create(new OnSubscribeFromIterable<>(new IterateOverFloat(10_000_000))) //
                        .flatMap(Observable::just)) //
                .observeOn(Schedulers.computation()) //
                .subscribe();
</pre></div>

goes through method `fastPath()` ?

`IterateOverFloat` is just a basic iterable that produces the asked number of floats.

I've tested with both RxJava-1.1.0 and 1.1.5 with the same results.

EDIT : ok, I have looked around a bit more, and here is what I have found.
I have created an Observable with backpressure support, which prints the first number of requested items:

<div class="highlight highlight-source-java"><pre>
Observable<Long> myObsWithBackPressureSupport = Observable.create(subs -> {
            subs.setProducer(new Producer() {

                AtomicBoolean bool = new AtomicBoolean(true);

                @Override
                public void request(long n) {
                    if(bool.getAndSet(false)){
                        System.out.println("request " + n);
                    }

                    LongStream.range(0, n).forEach(subs::onNext);
                }

            });
        });
</pre></div>


and I used it the following ways: 

<div class="highlight highlight-source-java"><pre>
// case 1
myObsWithBackPressureSupport //
        .observeOn(Schedulers.computation()) //
        .subscribe();

// case 2
myObsWithBackPressureSupport //
        .flatMap(l -> Observable.just(l)) //
        .observeOn(Schedulers.computation()) //
        .subscribe();
</pre></div>


In case 1, first request of 128 items.
In case 2, first request of Long.MAX_VALUE items.

I have concluded that using `flatMap` disables the backpressure support. Can someone tell me if I am right and if it is an intended behavior ?


